  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe `le()` (less than or equal) function in Float128.sol contains a subtle but important logical error in how it handles equality cases. While it does check for exact equality at the beginning of the function:\n\n```solidity\n// Line 899\nif (packedFloat.unwrap(a) == packedFloat.unwrap(b)) return true;\n```\n\nThe function has a flaw in its main comparison logic where numbers have equal exponents:\n[Link](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-04-forte/blob/4d6694f68e80543885da78666e38c0dc7052d992/src/Float128.sol#L930-L933)\n```solidity\n// Lines 929-935\nif and(iszero(aNeg), iszero(bNeg)) {  // Both numbers are positive\n    if eq(aExp, bExp) {\n        retVal := lt(aMan, bMan)  // ISSUE: Only checks \"less than\", not \"equal to\"\n    }\n    if lt(aExp, bExp) {\n        retVal := true\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis issue results in inconsistent comparison behavior when working with floating point numbers that are mathematically equal but have different bit representations. For example, the numbers `1000 * 10^3` and `1000000 * 10^0` both equal `1,000,000`, but when comparing these values, the function may return different results depending on which value is on which side of the comparison.\n\nThe impact of this issue includes:\n1. Inconsistent comparison results that break the expected mathematical properties of equality\n2. Potential vulnerabilities in financial calculations where exact comparisons are critical\n3. Logical inconsistencies between `le()` and `gt()` functions, where `!(a > b)` doesn't always equal `a <= b`\n\n## Recommended Mitigation\n\nThe issue can be fixed by modifying the `le()` function to properly handle equality cases in the main comparison logic, not just at the beginning of the function. Here's the recommended fix:\n\n```solidity\nif and(iszero(aNeg), iszero(bNeg)) {  // Both numbers are positive\n    if eq(aExp, bExp) {\n        retVal := lte(aMan, bMan)  // Use less than or equal instead of just less than\n    }\n    if lt(aExp, bExp) {\n        retVal := true\n    }\n}\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "## Proof of Concept\n\nThe following PoC demonstrates the issue by creating two numbers with the same mathematical value but different internal representations, and showing how the `le()` function fails to handle this properly:\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {Float128} from \"../src/Float128.sol\";\nimport {packedFloat} from \"../src/Types.sol\";\n\ncontract IncompleteEqualityHandlingPOC is Test {\n    using Float128 for packedFloat;\n    \n    // Constants from Float128.sol\n    uint256 constant MANTISSA_MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;\n    uint256 constant EXPONENT_MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00000000000000000000000000000000;\n    uint256 constant MANTISSA_SIGN_MASK = 0x80000000000000000000000000000000;\n    uint256 constant MANTISSA_L_FLAG_MASK = 0x40000000000000000000000000000000;\n    uint256 constant EXPONENT_BIT = 128;\n    \n    function setUp() public {}\n\n    function test_leEdgeCase() public {\n        // Create two numbers that should be mathematically equal but have different\n        // internal bit representations\n        uint aMan = 1000; // Mantissa for 'a'\n        uint aExp = 3;    // Exponent for 'a': 1000 * 10^3 = 1,000,000\n        \n        uint bMan = 1000000; // Mantissa for 'b'\n        uint bExp = 0;       // Exponent for 'b': 1,000,000 * 10^0 = 1,000,000\n        \n        // Manually construct the packedFloats (this avoids normalization)\n        packedFloat a = packedFloat.wrap(aMan | (aExp << EXPONENT_BIT));\n        packedFloat b = packedFloat.wrap(bMan | (bExp << EXPONENT_BIT));\n        \n        // Log the raw values to verify they're different\n        console.log(\"a raw:\", uint256(packedFloat.unwrap(a)));\n        console.log(\"b raw:\", uint256(packedFloat.unwrap(b)));\n        \n        // Verify they actually have different representations\n        assertTrue(\n            packedFloat.unwrap(a) != packedFloat.unwrap(b),\n            \"Values should have different internal representations\"\n        );\n        \n        // Now test the le() function behavior\n        bool aLeB = a.le(b);\n        bool bLeA = b.le(a);\n        \n        console.log(\"a <= b:\", aLeB);\n        console.log(\"b <= a:\", bLeA);\n        \n        // For mathematically equal values, both should be true\n        // But due to the issue in le(), one might be false\n        \n        // We expect at least one case to fail with the current implementation\n        if (!aLeB || !bLeA) {\n            console.log(\"FOUND ISSUE: le() function is not properly handling equal values with different representations\");\n        }\n        \n        // Also check consistency with gt() function\n        bool aGtB = a.gt(b);\n        bool bGtA = b.gt(a);\n        \n        console.log(\"a > b:\", aGtB);\n        console.log(\"b > a:\", bGtA);\n        \n        // For equal values, both should be false\n        if (aGtB || bGtA) {\n            console.log(\"FOUND ISSUE: gt() function is not properly handling equal values with different representations\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```\n[PASS] test_leEdgeCase() (gas: 12814)\nLogs:\n  a raw: 1020847100762815390390123822295304635368\n  b raw: 1000000\n  a <= b: false\n  b <= a: true\n  FOUND ISSUE: le() function is not properly handling equal values with different representations\n  a > b: true\n  b > a: false\n  FOUND ISSUE: gt() function is not properly handling equal values with different representations\n```\n\nThe logs clearly demonstrate the inconsistency:\n1. For mathematically equal values, `a <= b` returns `false` while `b <= a` returns `true`\n2. For the same values, `a > b` returns `true` while `b > a` returns `false`",
